# 24. Incident Timeline

Every investigation gets a chronological timeline.

Example:

```text
14:05  Deployment started
14:07  Deployment completed
14:11  Error rate +5%
14:18  Database connections +40%
14:25  HTTP 500 +15%
14:32  Incident detected
14:37  Investigation started
14:44  Hypothesis created
14:51  Failure reproduced
15:02  Fix deployed
15:08  Validation passed
15:15  Incident resolved
```

This creates a **time-machine view of the incident**.

---

# 25. Testing Strategy

FaultLens will not rely only on manual testing.

### Unit tests

Test individual functions and services.

### Integration tests

Test:

```text
API
+
Database
+
Redis
```

### End-to-end tests

Test complete workflows:

```text
Login
 ↓
Create incident
 ↓
Investigate
 ↓
Generate evidence
 ↓
Run experiment
 ↓
Validate fix
 ↓
Resolve incident
```

### Load testing

Measure:

* API throughput
* Telemetry ingestion
* Queue processing
* Database performance
* WebSocket connections

---

# 26. Observability of FaultLens Itself

A reliability platform should also be observable.

FaultLens will expose its own:

```text
Request latency
Error rates
Queue depth
Worker processing time
Database latency
WebSocket connections
Job failures
```

This creates a useful engineering principle:

> **The system built to investigate failures should itself be observable.**

---

# 27. Deployment

The application should be designed to run locally using Docker.

```text
Docker Compose

┌─────────────┐
│ Web         │
├─────────────┤
│ API         │
├─────────────┤
│ Worker      │
├─────────────┤
│ PostgreSQL  │
├─────────────┤
│ Redis       │
└─────────────┘
```

The project will also target free-tier deployment where practical.

AI functionality must remain optional so that the deployed application can operate without paid AI APIs.

